# PACKAGE AN APPLICATION, THEN RUN IT. The one test that exercises the whole
# feature the way a user does: a directory in, an executable out, and that
# executable started from somewhere that is not the application directory and
# told nothing about where it came from. Everything else in Packaging/ tests a
# piece. Every RUN line runs from lit's output directory, which is deliberately
# NOT the fixture: an application that only starts next to its own source
# files has not packaged anything.
#
# WHAT A PASS ACTUALLY PROVES, which is more than "it exited 0": run_bundle
# sets require_script_images, so the launcher REFUSES TO START if any of the
# page's scripts had to be compiled from source - the failure this whole
# project treats as the worst one because nothing else reports it. The
# blinded arms are the second half: the same launcher with nothing appended,
# handed an HTML file, and handed the two bundles ctcompile will not produce
# and a future packager might (ctcompile-test-app_bundle writes them) must all
# refuse - the guard is in run_app, behind a window, and nothing else in the
# suite gets that far.
#
# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t && ctcompile-test-app_bundle %t
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctcompile %S/app --entry index.html --launcher ctrun -o %t/app --verbose --fonts %{monorepo}/ctbrowser/resources/fonts --manifest %t/app.json 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PACKAGED
# RUN: test -x %t/app
#
# IT SAID WHAT IT DID, and the numbers are checked rather than the phrasing:
# two scripts is the count that matters, because a packager that quietly
# packed one produces an application that starts and is refused for a reason
# pointing at the launcher. greeting.js and app.css are named by the document,
# not by the command line, and late.json is the one a script asked for AFTER
# the page was up: fetch queues its request and it is drained from a tick, so
# this name exists only if the packager let the page RUN before asking what it
# wanted - the difference between packaging a document and an application.
# PACKAGED-DAG: 2 scripts compiled
# PACKAGED-DAG: asset  greeting.js
# PACKAGED-DAG: asset  app.css
# PACKAGED-DAG: asset  late.json
#
# Opening a file is not proof that its bytes reached the destination. Linux's
# full device deterministically rejects the write/close while permitting open.
# RUN: not ctcompile %S/app --entry index.html --fonts %{monorepo}/ctbrowser/resources/fonts --bundle -o /dev/full 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FULL
# RUN: not ctcompile %S/app --entry index.html --fonts %{monorepo}/ctbrowser/resources/fonts --bundle -o %t/failed.ctapp --manifest /dev/full 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FULL
# FULL: cannot write{{.*}}/dev/full
#
# THE MANIFEST, PARSED RATHER THAN MATCHED: a grep for a field name would pass
# on a document no parser accepts, so json.tool reads it first and FileCheck
# reads json.tool's normalised output. The faces are under a name of the
# compiler's own, not the build machine's path; and every program id distinct,
# because two scripts sharing one could not be told apart at lookup.
# RUN: python3 -m json.tool %t/app.json | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MANIFEST
# MANIFEST-DAG: "entry": "index.html"
# MANIFEST-DAG: "font_directory": "fonts"
# MANIFEST-DAG: "program_id": "[[ID:[^"]+]]"
# MANIFEST-NOT: "program_id": "[[ID]]"
# RUN: python3 -c 'import json, sys; m = json.load(open(sys.argv[1])); sys.exit(len(m["scripts"]) != 2)' %t/app.json
# RUN: test $(stat -c %s %t/app) -gt $(stat -c %s ctrun)
#
# ---- run it, from somewhere that is not the application directory ----------
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 %t/app
# A PACKAGED APPLICATION CAN SAY WHAT IT IS: --info prints its manifest and
# does not start the application, which is what it used to do with any argument.
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy %t/app --info | python3 -m json.tool | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=INFO
# INFO: "entry": "index.html"
#
# ---- the blinded arms -------------------------------------------------------
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctrun
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctrun %S/app/index.html 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=NOT-A-BUNDLE
# NOT-A-BUNDLE: not a ctbrowser application bundle
#
# ---- and it must look the SAME as the page it came from ---------------------
# THE CHECK THAT CAUGHT THE FONTS. A packaged application is sealed - it answers
# from what it carries and never from the disk - and the vendored faces are
# loaded THROUGH the asset registry, so the first sealed build silently dropped
# to the built-in bitmap font: it exited 0, it rendered, and it just looked
# worse. So the SAME page is rendered twice with real fonts - once from source
# with CTBROWSER_FONT_PATH, once packaged with NO font path and a working
# directory that is not the application's, which is the only way this can see
# the bundle doing the work - and the bytes compared. It is deliberately last,
# because a byte comparison of two renders catches anything the packaging
# lost, not only fonts. It proves less on a build without TTF support: both
# arms fall back and match.
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONT_PATH=%{monorepo}/ctbrowser/resources/fonts CTBROWSER_SCREENSHOT=%t/from-source.ppm ctbrowse %S/app/index.html
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_SCREENSHOT=%t/packaged.ppm %t/app
# RUN: cmp %t/from-source.ppm %t/packaged.ppm
#
# ---- what the compiler must refuse to package -------------------------------
# A PAGE OF MODULE SCRIPTS. Nothing here can compile one ahead of time, so
# packaging it would produce an application whose only property is that it is
# exactly as slow as it was before, every count in the packager reading a
# truthful zero - the silent failure this tool exists to avoid.
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctcompile %S/app-module --entry index.html --launcher ctrun -o %t/app-module 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MODULE
# MODULE: module script
#
# ---- and what the launcher must refuse to run -------------------------------
# RUN: test -f %t/no-images.ctapp && test -f %t/module-page.ctapp
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctrun %t/no-images.ctapp
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctrun %t/module-page.ctapp
